s necessary and sufficient for $A$ and $B$ to be disjoint?","options":["$A\\cup B=\\varnothing$","$\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)=0$","$A\\cap B=\\varnothing$","$\\mathbb{P}(A)+\\mathbb{P}(B)\\le1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"73c85f861ef277fd9b5604e5","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch2_disjoint_events_005_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $A$ and $B$ be disjoint events. When are they also independent?","options":["Exactly when $\\mathbb{P}(A)=0$ or $\\mathbb{P}(B)=0$","Always, because neither event affects the other","Never, whatever their probabilities are","Exactly when $\\mathbb{P}(A)=\\mathbb{P}(B)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f9f4c008690e2c2abd0e0c23","time_limit":30},{"id":"pt_batch2_disjoint_events_019_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"On one die roll, let $A=\\{1,2\\}$, $B=\\{2,3\\}$ and $C=\\{1,3\\}$, so $A\\cap B\\cap C=\\varnothing$. Which statement is correct?","options":["No two of these events are disjoint, so they are not disjoint","The three events are disjoint, since no outcome lies in all three","For a fair die, $\\mathbb{P}(A\\cup B\\cup C)=\\mathbb{P}(A)+\\mathbb{P}(B)+\\mathbb{P}(C)$","Exactly one of the pairs $(A,B)$, $(B,C)$, $(A,C)$ is disjoint"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"721429cc355718f28b7d1efa","time_limit":40}]}}